    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d100-a3">

                <pb facs="0245" n="149" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg149"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure 3—Translation<anchor n="79"
                            xml:id="d100-f1"/>]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Envoy Extraordinary at Damascus</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Alype</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">of the French High
                        Commissioner to the States of Syria and the Lebanon</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">De Jouvenel</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Dean of
                        the Consular Corps at Damascus</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Keeley</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Damascus</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-05-14"><hi rendition="#italic">May 14,
                        1926</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 248/SP</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Dean</hi>: Referring to your letter of April
                    28th, I have the honor to address to you herewith the reply that General
                    Vallier, in charge of the maintenance of public order and security in the region
                    of Damascus, has transmitted to me.</p>
                <p>I am persuaded that the information given by the General will allay the anxiety
                    of the Consular Corps of this city.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pierre Alype</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
